Box-Sizing : Border-box is preferred over the content-box because it provides more predictable and responsive layout. 
To set all the elements Box-Sizing to border-box, we can use the universal selector (*). It is the old and easy way to set
Box-Sizing to border-box for all elements. 
*{
  Box-Sizing: border-box;
}

The problem with this method is in the way in which the universal selector works. It selects all the elements amd applies the 
CSS to them unless they are explicitly overriden witb some other CSS later. 

Suppose, if we have a container or element which we want to style in content-box for a project's need. 
Ex- HTML 
<div id="container">
   <div id="child">
      This is child element.
   </div>
</div>

CSS
#container {
   Box-Sizing: content-box;
    }
We may expect the child element of this container to have the same styling as content-box, but the problem is the universal selector 
has already set its Box-Sizing to border-box and it will remain the same until we change it.


THE SOLUTION TO THIS IS Box-Sizing: inherit;

html{
 Box-Sizing: border-box;
}

*, *::before, *::after {
     Box-Sizing: inherit;
}

Using these lines of code in our CSS files at first sets all the HTML elements to border-box. 
Now the second half of this code block implies that all the elements who have a change in their 
Box-Sizing will inherit the same Box-Sizing to its child elements.

So, if there's a requirement to set the Box-Sizing of an element to content-box, then its child elements will automatically inherit 
the same Box-Sizing property of content box, so we don't have to manually do it again for each child element. 
This prevents the unintentional or unexpected styling behaviour. 

